---
import Landing from '../layouts/Landing.astro';
import type { Faq } from '../lib/schema';

const faqs: Faq[] = [
  {
    q: 'What is a one-time secret link?',
    a: 'A link to a piece of text, such as a password or a private message, that can be opened once. As soon as it has been read, the text is deleted and the link stops working.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I let a link be opened more than once?',
    a: 'Yes. Choose up to 10 views. The note is destroyed after the last one, or when it expires, whichever comes first.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does a link last if nobody opens it?',
    a: 'From this page, 1 hour, 1 day or 7 days. With an account you can choose longer, up to 12 months on the Business plan. Expired notes can’t be opened and are deleted within the hour.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I cancel a link I’ve already sent?',
    a: 'Yes. When you create a note you also get a private destroy link. Open it and confirm, and the note is deleted at once.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I find out when it has been read?',
    a: 'Yes. Tick “Email me when it’s read” and we’ll send you an email when the note is opened. We keep your address only until the note is destroyed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the link itself secret?',
    a: 'Treat it as secret. The key that decrypts the note is part of the link, so anyone who has the whole link can open the note once. Add a password, sent another way, if the link might be seen by someone else.',
  },
];
---

<Landing
  title="One-time secret links that self-destruct"
  description="Create a one-time secret link: share a password or private message that can be read once, then is deleted. End-to-end encrypted, free, no sign-up."
  eyebrow="One-time secret links"
  heading="A secret link that works once, then destroys itself."
  lead="Write your secret, get a link, send it. When it’s opened, the secret is deleted and the link stops working. It’s encrypted in your browser first, so we can’t read it."
  points={[
    'Readable once, or up to 10 times if you choose',
    'Deleted after 1 hour, 1 day or 7 days if nobody opens it',
    'Optional password and read notification',
  ]}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <h2>How a one-time link works</h2>
  <p>
    When you press <strong>Create secure link</strong>, your browser makes a random 256-bit key and encrypts the secret
    with it (AES-256-GCM). We receive only the encrypted text and store it under a random ID. The link you get looks like
    this:
  </p>
  <pre><code>https://vanishnote.net/n/ID#KEY</code></pre>
  <p>
    The <code>ID</code> tells us which note to fetch. The <code>KEY</code> after the <code>#</code> decrypts it, and
    browsers never send that part to a server. So the key goes from you to your recipient without passing through us.
  </p>
  <p>
    When the recipient presses Reveal, we hand over the encrypted note and delete it in the same database step. Two people
    can’t both open a one-time note, even at the same moment. Their browser then decrypts it.
  </p>

  <h2>When to use one</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Passwords, PINs and recovery codes. See <a href="/send-password-securely">sending a password securely</a>.</li>
    <li>API keys, tokens and <code>.env</code> files. See <a href="/share-api-keys">sharing API keys</a>.</li>
    <li>Bank details, ID numbers, door codes and Wi-Fi passwords.</li>
    <li>Anything you’d rather not leave sitting in an inbox or a chat history.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What a one-time link can’t do</h2>
  <p>
    It can’t stop the recipient from copying what they read, and it can’t protect a device that’s already compromised.
    Anyone holding the whole link can open the note, so add a password for anything that matters, and send it a different
    way. Our <a href="/security">security model</a> lists the limits in full.
  </p>
</Landing>
